{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And you shall serve.</b> The sense: you shall not serve any but HaShem, who brought you out from the house of bondage and brought you into the land of the nations who served other elohim, and they could not save them from your hand. And the service of HaShem is to do all that He commanded, to love Him and to cleave to Him, and to swear by His name, and to pray to Him, and to slaughter before Him the regular and additional offerings, and to honor Him by giving tithes. Behold, his reward is with Him, and they are four things in this world: the first, \"and I will bless your bread and your water\"; the second, \"and I will remove illness from your midst\"; the third, \"there shall be no miscarrying or barren woman in your land\"; the fourth, \"the number of your days I will fill.\" Behold, all these concern the human body. Moreover, the opposite of these blessings I will do to all your enemies, namely, \"I will send my terror\" and also \"the hornet.\"" "<b>Said Abraham the author:</b> There are great sages, sages of the two Torot, who did not engage in the wisdom of generation [nature]; therefore I cannot explain these blessings without mentioning a little of the sciences, because the human body is from the lower world, and the supernal soul is bound to it. There are intermediaries between the soul and the body — behold, they are two forces, and in the holy tongue they are mostly called רוח (spirit) and נפש (soul). For the soul [נשמה] is wisdom, and its seat is in the brain of the head, and from it comes the power of all sensations and the movement of desire. The רוח (spirit) is in the heart, and in it is the life of man, and it seeks dominion to overcome all that stands against it, and it is the possessor of anger — and so Solomon said: \"Do not hasten in your רוח to be angry\" (Ecclesiastes 7:9), \"all his רוח a fool lets out\" (Proverbs 29:11). And the נפש (soul) is in the liver, and it is what desires to eat, and thus it is written: \"when your נפש desires\" (Deuteronomy 12:20), and the lust of intercourse comes from it. Behold, there is a person created such that these three mentioned are strong, or all weak, or this one strong and this one weak, or neither strong nor weak. Behold, human beings are divided into all these types. And the honored Name gave the Torah to strengthen, to intensify, and to magnify the supernal soul, and then the body will not rule over it. And if the Torah is not kept, then the body will overpower the soul. Behold, I will give you a parable. We know, according to the wisdom of nature, that every man in whom red bile predominates by his nature will be quick-tempered, for such is the nature of fire, for red bile resembles it. And behold, the soul pursues the body when it is not wise enough to endure it. Let us also imagine a man who has no heat in his body, and another comes and insults and curses him until he becomes angry — behold, he generated in his body a heat that was not there before his anger. And HaShem, exalted, for the sake of His lovingkindness, chose Israel and taught them His Torah. And when they keep it, they will find wisdom, and He will guide them on a straight path in everything that will not harm them. The principle is that their body be subject to the obedience of the soul and not the reverse, that the soul be subject to the obedience of the body. And as the soul exerts itself, the power of the force that guards the body — which a person receives from the heavens, and which is called תולדת (nature) — will also exert itself. Behold, the blessing will be upon all food and drink, for there is a curse that comes to diminish the power of nature, in the manner of \"you will eat and not be satisfied\" (Micah 6:14). Behold, all illnesses come on account of food entering the body; behold, one who keeps the Torah need not fear them — this is \"and I will bless your bread.\" And there are illnesses that come from outside the body on account of changes in the air according to the variation of the arrangement of the ministers [celestial bodies]; concerning this it is written: \"and I will remove illness from your midst.\" Behold, one who keeps the Torah has no need for a physician alongside the honored Name; therefore it is written: \"even in his illness he did not seek HaShem but the physicians\" (II Chronicles 16:12). Behold, I have spoken on the wisdom of medicine; now I will speak on the wisdom of the constellations.\n\nKnow that everything born — they call it מקרים (accidents), in the manner of what the arrangement of the ministers [celestial bodies] indicates at the moment of birth, and the relation of these to those, and the matter is very deep. Behold, according to a woman's birth-chart she may be משכלה (miscarrying), for the nature of the womb is to miscarry the offspring, or she may be עקרה (barren) and not give birth. Behold, if the one who cleaves to HaShem has, according to his nature, that he will not beget sons — the honored Name, if he cleaves to Him, will strengthen the power of the nature of the kidneys and correct the operations of the seed so that he begets sons. Therefore HaShem said to Abraham: \"I am El Shaddai; walk before Me and be whole\" (Genesis 17:1). This is what our predecessors of blessed memory said: \"Leave your astrology\" — and if you cut a covenant with Me, that you will serve Me, I will multiply you exceedingly, for I will overcome the nature. And this is not overcoming the arrangement of the stars, for they were not created to do good or evil to the lower world; rather, they always proceed to the service of HaShem for their own needs, and in their course the inhabitants of the lower world receive a thing and its opposite through them. Behold, if we keep [the Torah], we will not receive [evil]. And I will speak further on this in the pericope of כי תשא. Behold, the third blessing is that each of them will beget children, and they will not die in the lifetime of their fathers."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The phrase 'ועבדתם את יהוה אלהיכם' (Exodus 23:25) is the lemma head; only the first word is given bold. Ibn Ezra's comment paraphrases the sense of the entire verse before glossing 'his reward is with Him' (שכרו אתו) from the end of the verse.", 1 "The comment addresses all four promised blessings of Exodus 23:25–26. 'The wisdom of generation' (חכמת התולדות) refers to natural science/physiology; תולדת in the anatomical passages means 'nature' or 'innate force.' The passage is one continuous comment; the internal section break before 'Now I will speak on the wisdom of the constellations' reflects Ibn Ezra's own transition, not a manuscript division. 'The two Torot' (השתי תורות) likely refers to Written and Oral Torah."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:38:09.648451190Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}